         <div type="bio">
            <p>Poet, novelist and publisher. He succeeded Ezra Pound as foreign editor of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Little Review</name> in 1919. With his then-wife Mary Butts he started the short-lived Ovid Press in London which issued editions of Pound and T. S. Eliot. Rodker met JJ in Paris in July 1920. After Shakespeare and Company’s first edition of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Ulysses</name> in 1922, he published a second edition in October 1922 for the Egoist Press of London and a third edition in January 1923. Rodker contributed an essay on the future <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Finnegans Wake</name> to <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Our Exagmination Round His Factification for Incamination of Work in Progress</name> (1929). <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
